THIRD-PARTY SOFTWARE
====================

What Caterva distributes depends on the artifact. The source, the wheel and
the sdist do not bundle, vendor or redistribute any Python dependency:
`requirements.txt` names them; pip fetches them from PyPI onto the user's
own machine. That fact does most of the work below, because almost every
obligation in an open-source licence attaches on DISTRIBUTION, and
distributing source that NAMES a dependency is not distributing the
dependency.

Two artifacts are different, and this file says so rather than saying
"nothing is bundled":

  * The app folders and Caterva.app (since v0.3.0 and v0.5.0) carry the
    Python runtime and the Python libraries (see "WHAT IS ACTUALLY
    DISTRIBUTED" below).
  * Caterva Studio's page, which Caterva.app carries (and a checkout can
    build), bundles JavaScript packages and three typefaces: React, d3,
    Radix UI, markdown-it, lucide-react, Recharts and the rest of the 88
    packages the build found in the page, under MIT, ISC, BSD, 0BSD and the
    Unlicense, and the typefaces Spectral, Atkinson Hyperlegible Next and DM
    Mono under the SIL Open Font License 1.1. Their licence texts are in
    `licenses/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt`, written by the page's build from its
    own module graph (the build fails if a bundled package has no licence
    text or a licence outside that list), and the three typefaces' texts, copied
    unmodified from their packages, sit beside it. Inside Caterva.app they
    are under `Contents/Resources/caterva/_internal/caterva/studio/static/
    licenses/`, and Help, Licences opens that folder, the Python
    components' `licenses/` folder and this file.

