python-libsbml — LGPL-2.1-or-later
----------------------------------

libSBML is copyrighted jointly by the California Institute of Technology,
the EMBL European Bioinformatics Institute, the University of Heidelberg and
others, and licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version
2.1 or any later version.

The LGPL permits a work to USE the library without that work becoming LGPL,
which is the whole point of the "Lesser" in its name. Its obligations —
notice, and the ability for a recipient to relink against a modified
libSBML — attach when you convey the library itself.

Whether Caterva conveys it depends on which artifact you hold, and the
section "WHAT IS ACTUALLY DISTRIBUTED" below says so artifact by artifact.
From 2026-08-15 to 2026-09-19 the answer was "never": one CI workflow,
`tests.yml`, publishing nothing. Since v0.2.0 the wheel and sdist name
libSBML without containing it. Since v0.3.0 (2026-09-21) a second workflow,
`release.yml`, publishes a downloadable app folder that DOES contain it, and
the obligations are met in the way that section describes.

THE TRIGGER THAT CHANGES THIS

  `.devcontainer/Dockerfile` runs `pip install -r requirements-dev.txt`, so
  an image built from it CONTAINS libSBML. Building that image locally is
  private use and unrestricted. **Publishing it — to Docker Hub, GHCR, or
  anywhere a third party can pull it — is conveying an LGPL library**, and
  the notice and relinking obligations attach at that moment.

  The same applies to any wheel, installer or bundle that vendors it.

  `scripts/check_release_artifacts.py` fails the build if a workflow that
  publishes is added while this NOTICE lacks the LGPL conveyance section.
  That guard exists so the obligation is not discovered after the fact.

  WHAT ELSE ATTACHES AT THAT MOMENT

  The section above names libSBML because that is the dependency Caterva
  installs. A published image also conveys **its base image**, and that is
  a separate set of licences nobody put in this file.

  Caterva's Dockerfiles start from:

      FROM node:22-alpine       (Dockerfile, both stages)
      FROM python:3.12-slim     (.devcontainer/Dockerfile)

  Alpine ships **busybox, which is GPL-2.0**. Debian slim ships coreutils
  and bash, which are **GPL-3.0**, over **glibc, which is LGPL-2.1**. None
  of that is unusual and none of it is a reason to change base image —
  publishing container images built on official bases is ordinary practice,
  and the obligations are ordinary too. But they are obligations, they are
  not the same ones as libSBML's, and "we checked the Python dependencies"
  is not the same as "we checked the image".

  This is recorded rather than resolved because **nothing is published**, so
  nothing attaches yet. The point of writing it down now is that the person
  who runs `docker push` will be looking at the section above, which names
  one library, and would reasonably conclude the analysis was complete.

  Before publishing an image, someone should read the licence manifests of
  the specific base image tags in use — `docker run --rm <image> sh -c 'cat
  /usr/share/doc/*/copyright'` on the Debian one, and Alpine's
  `/lib/apk/db/installed` on the other. That is a real task, not a formality,
  and it has not been done.


