  contain NO third-party library. They NAME python-libsbml (LGPL-2.1-or-later) as a dependency in
  their metadata, and pip fetches it from PyPI onto the user's machine at
  install time, exactly as `requirements.txt` did before. Distributing a
  package that names a dependency is not conveying the dependency; the
  LGPL-2.1 conveyance obligations (notice, and the recipient's ability to
  relink against a modified libSBML) therefore do not attach to these two
  artifacts. Verified on every build by `scripts/build_release.py`, which
  lists the wheel and refuses one containing a `.so`, a test, or anything
  outside `caterva/`.

  2. THE APP FOLDER, ONE PER PLATFORM (since v0.3.0). `caterva-<version>-
  <os>-<arch>.tar.gz` / `.zip` is a PyInstaller ONEDIR freeze of the
  released wheel: the Python runtime, Caterva, and every library Caterva
  imports. This artifact CONVEYS libSBML, and not once: python-libsbml's
  extension is the library as a separate file, and libroadrunner's
  `_roadrunner` extension and Antimony's `libantimony` shared library each
  carry their OWN statically linked copy, put there by their upstream
  projects (measured on the build machine: 5.21.1, 5.20.4 and 5.20.2).
  `scripts/build_app.py` scans every binary it collects for the library,
  writes each copy, its version and whether it is replaceable into the
  folder's README.txt and beside the archive, and refuses the folder if
  python-libsbml's copy is not a separate file. The obligations, and how
  each is met:

    notice        README.txt names libSBML, its licence, and every file
                  that carries it. licenses/python-libsbml/ carries
                  libSBML's own LICENSE.txt (copyright and distribution
                  terms) and the full text of the GNU LGPL v2.1, which that
                  file refers to and the wheel omits.

    relinking     The freeze is ONEDIR, never onefile. python-libsbml's
                  extension (`_libsbml.<abi>.so` / `.pyd`) is one separate
                  file under `_internal/libsbml/`, loaded at run time by the
                  interpreter's import mechanism, not compiled into the
                  executable; a recipient replaces it by putting an
                  interface-compatible build for the same Python version
                  and platform at the same path, which README.txt names.
                  That is the second half of what LGPL-2.1 §6(b) asks (the
                  work "will operate properly with a modified version of
                  the library"); it is NOT the first half (a copy "already
                  present on the user's computer system"), because this
                  folder is what puts the copy there. The copies inside
                  `_roadrunner` and `libantimony` are not separately
                  replaceable at all. So the project does not rest on
                  §6(b). It rests on offering the corresponding source from
                  the same place (§4, §6(d)): the release workflow fetches
                  the source archive of every libSBML version the folders
                  report, and of libroadrunner and Antimony at the pinned
                  versions, and attaches them to the release page beside
                  the folders, covered by the same SHA256SUMS. A folder is
                  not published if a source download fails.

                  Whether a shipped-but-replaceable copy is a §6(b)
                  mechanism, and whether upstream source attached to the
                  same page satisfies "same place" for copies that
                  upstream wheels embed, are the two questions to put to
                  counsel; the folder is built so that the answer to either
                  does not have to be yes for the obligations to be met.

    signing       Not signed, not notarised (below).

  The folder conveys other software too, each under its own terms, each
